7. The river is full, by which they say it is drinkable;
The barley is grown, by which they say it is hidden;
The one gone far, by which they say he is called;
He has come to you, now take and eat, O Brahmin.

He wrote this verse on a palm leaf.

Brahmin. Because of which bird. Full of water. The river also. 'Drinkable' (peyyaṃ), meaning a river with water that can be drunk while standing on the bank. They say. Because of which bird. Grown. The young grain such as barley also. 'Hidden' (guyhaṃ), meaning a grain crop that is already able to conceal. They say. Because of which bird. To a far place. The friend who has gone. They say will arrive. That bird. For you. Has been sent. Here, take it. That bird's meat. Eat.

The Blessed One arrived and asked, "Monks, with what talk are you sitting gathered here now?" When they replied, "With such and such talk," he said, "Monks, not only now is the Tathāgata wise and skillful in means; in the past also, the Tathāgata was indeed wise and skillful in means," and he brought the past story.

Story of the Past: Monks, in the past, when King Brahmadatta was reigning in Bārāṇasī, the Bodhisatta was born into a chaplain's (purohita) family. Upon reaching maturity, he acquired all the arts in Takkasilā. After his father's passing, he obtained the position of chaplain and became the minister who advised the King of Bārāṇasī on matters of welfare and Dhamma.

Later, the King, listening to the words of slanderers, became angry with the Bodhisatta and banished him from Bārāṇasī, saying, "Do not live in my presence." The Bodhisatta took his wife and children and lived in a small village in the kingdom of Kāsi.
